 \n0000002742 00000 n \n0000003597 00000 n \n0000003698 00000 n \n0000003910 00000 n \n0000004098 00000 n \ntrailer\n<<\n/Size 13\n/Root 12 0 R\n/Info 11 0 R\n>>\nstartxref\n4202\n%%EOF\n','2026-03-26 16:26:29','everyone','2026-03-26 10:56:29','[{\"question\": \"How does a neuron generate an action potential?\", \"options\": [\"By the diffusion of ions through the cell membrane\", \"By the release of neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft\", \"By the breakdown of ATP to release energy\", \"By the reuptake of ions into the neuron\"], \"correct_answer\": \"A\", \"explanation\": \"An action potential in a neuron is generated by the rapid influx of sodium ions through voltage-gated channels, which changes the membrane potential.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"In a neuron, where does the action potential start?\", \"options\": [\"Dendrites\", \"Cell body\", \"Axon\", \"Synaptic vesicles\"], \"correct_answer\": \"B\", \"explanation\": \"The action potential begins at the cell body and propagates down the axon.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following statements is true regarding neurotransmitter release at the synapse?\", \"options\": [\"Neurotransmitters are continuously released into the synaptic cleft\", \"Neurotransmitters are released in large quantities for each synapse\", \"Neurotransmitters are released in a small quantity and reused\", \"Neurotransmitters are released and immediately inactivated\"], \"correct_answer\": \"C\", \"explanation\": \"Neurotransmitters are released in small quantities, bind to receptors on the postsynaptic neuron, and are then either inactivated by enzymes or reuptaken by the presynaptic neuron for reuse.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of an action potential in a neuron?\", \"options\": [\"It involves the active transport of ions\", \"It is a brief, all-or-none electrical signal\", \"It requires ATP for its propagation\", \"It decreases the resting membrane potential\"], \"correct_answer\": \"B\", \"explanation\": \"An action potential is a brief, all-or-none electrical signal that travels along the axon and involves the influx of sodium ions, causing a rapid change in membrane potential.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"In synaptic transmission, what is the role of the postsynaptic neuron?\", \"options\": [\"It generates the action potential\", \"It receives the neurotransmitter signal\", \"It synthesizes neurotransmitters\", \"It breaks down neurotransmitters\"], \"correct_answer\": \"B\", \"explanation\": \"The postsynaptic neuron receives the neurotransmitter signal and can either activate ion channels or phosphorylate proteins, depending on the neurotransmitter type.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following is not a part of the neuron\'s structure?\", \"options\": [\"Dendrites\", \"Axon\", \"Synaptic vesicles\", \"Soma\"], \"correct_answer\": \"C\", \"explanation\": \"Synaptic vesicles are found in the synaptic cleft, not within the neuron itself.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"What is the immediate consequence of a neurotransmitter binding to a receptor on the postsynaptic neuron?\", \"options\": [\"Membrane potential changes\", \"Action potential initiation\", \"Neurotransmitter synthesis\", \"Neurotransmitter degradation\"], \"correct_answer\": \"A\", \"explanation\": \"Binding of a neurotransmitter to a receptor on the postsynaptic neuron typically results in a change in the membrane potential of the postsynaptic neuron, leading to further neuronal activity or inhibition.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following statements is true regarding the mechanism of action potentials in neurons?\", \"options\": [\"It involves the flow of ions and changes in membrane potential\", \"It is caused by the release of neurotransmitters\", \"It is a passive process\", \"It involves the breakdown of ATP\"], \"correct_answer\": \"A\", \"explanation\": \"The generation of an action potential in a neuron involves the flow of ions through voltage-gated channels, which results in a change in the membrane potential.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"In synaptic transmission, what is the role of the presynaptic neuron?\", \"options\": [\"It receives the neurotransmitter signal\", \"It synthesizes neurotransmitters\", \"It generates the action potential\", \"It breaks down neurotransmitters\"], \"correct_answer\": \"B\", \"explanation\": \"The presynaptic neuron synthesizes neurotransmitters and releases them into the synaptic cleft, which then binds to receptors on the postsynaptic neuron.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"What is the role of the synaptic cleft in synaptic transmission?\", \"options\": [\"It stores neurotransmitters\", \"It allows for the diffusion of ions\", \"It acts as a barrier to prevent action potentials\", \"It is the space where neurotransmitters are released and received\"], \"correct_answer\": \"D\", \"explanation\": \"The synaptic cleft is the space between the presynaptic and postsynaptic neurons where neurotransmitters are released and received.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following best describes the process of neurotransmitter reuptake?\", \"options\": [\"It is the process of neurotransmitters being broken down by enzymes\", \"It is the process of neurotransmitters being released into the synaptic cleft\", \"It is the process of neurotransmitters being transported back into the presynaptic neuron\", \"It is the process of neurotransmitters being degraded by the postsynaptic neuron\"], \"correct_answer\": \"C\", \"explanation\": \"Neurotransmitter reuptake is the process of the neurotransmitter being transported back into the presynaptic neuron after it has bound to receptors on the postsynaptic neuron.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following is not a part of the neuron\'s structure?\", \"options\": [\"Soma\", \"Axon\", \"Synaptic vesicles\", \"Dendrites\"], \"correct_answer\": \"C\", \"explanation\": \"Synaptic vesicles are found in the synaptic cleft, not within the neuron itself.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"What is the function of the postsynaptic membrane in synaptic transmission?\", \"options\": [\"It generates action potentials\", \"It receives neurotransmitter signals\", \"It synthesizes neurotransmitters\", \"It breaks down neurotransmitters\"], \"correct_answer\": \"B\", \"explanation\": \"The postsynaptic membrane receives neurotransmitter signals and can either activate ion channels or phosphorylate proteins, depending on the neurotransmitter type.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following is not a part of the neuron\'s structure?\", \"options\": [\"Soma\", \"Axon\", \"Synaptic vesicles\", \"Sodium channels\"], \"correct_answer\": \"C\", \"explanation\": \"Synaptic vesicles are found in the synaptic cleft, not within the neuron itself.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"What is the immediate consequence of neurotransmitter binding to a receptor?\", \"options\": [\"Membrane potential changes\", \"Action potential initiation\", \"Neurotransmitter synthesis\", \"Neurotransmitter degradation\"], \"correct_answer\": \"A\", \"explanation\": \"Binding of a neurotransmitter to a receptor on the postsynaptic neuron typically results in a change in the membrane potential of the postsynaptic neuron.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of synaptic transmission?\", \"options\": [\"It is a passive process\", \"It involves the active transport of ions\", \"It requires ATP for its propagation\", \"It involves the diffusion of ions and changes in membrane potential\"], \"correct_answer\": \"D\", \"explanation\": \"Synaptic transmission involves the diffusion of ions through receptors on the postsynaptic neuron, which results in a change in the membrane potential.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"What is the role of the presynaptic neuron in synaptic transmission?\", \"options\": [\"It generates the action potential\", \"It releases neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft\", \"It receives neurotransmitter signals\", \"It breaks down neurotransmitters\"], \"correct_answer\": \"B\", \"explanation\": \"The presynaptic neuron releases neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft and synthesizes them in the first place.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following is not a part of the neuron\'s structure?\", \"options\": [\"Soma\", \"Axon\", \"Synaptic vesicles\", \"Dendrites\"], \"correct_answer\": \"C\", \"explanation\": \"Synaptic vesicles are found in the synaptic cleft, not within the neuron itself.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"What is the immediate consequence of neurotransmitter binding to a receptor?\", \"options\": [\"Membrane potential changes\", \"Action potential initiation\", \"Neurotransmitter synthesis\", \"Neurotransmitter degradation\"], \"correct_answer\": \"A\", \"explanation\": \"Binding of a neurotransmitter to a receptor on the postsynaptic neuron typically results in a change in the membrane potential of the postsynaptic neuron.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}, {\"question\": \"Which of the following is not a part of the neuron\'s structure?\", \"options\": [\"Soma\", \"Axon\", \"Synaptic vesicles\", \"Dendrites\"], \"correct_answer\": \"C\", \"explanation\": \"Synaptic vesicles are found in the synaptic cleft, not within the neuron itself.\", \"type\": \"mcq\"}]'),(5,'2026-03-30','9','1.maths - probability, circles, introduction to trigonometry, coordinate geometry, arithmetic progressions, polynomials, real numbers\n2.science - our environment, sources of energy, electricity, light ,  reflection and refraction, heredity and evolution, how do organisms reproduce?, control and coordination, life processes, carbon and its compounds, metals and non-metals, acids, bases and salts, chemical reactions and equations, chemical reaction